  "title": "How to Substitute into and Rearrange Formulae",
  "content": "<p><strong>Substitution</strong> means replacing letters with given values and calculating the result. Follow BIDMAS carefully when evaluating.</p><p><strong>Writing formulae:</strong> Translate word problems into algebraic expressions. Identify the subject (the letter on its own on one side).</p><p><strong>Rearranging formulae:</strong> Use inverse operations to isolate a different variable. Whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other. Think of it as ‘undoing’ operations in reverse order.</p><p>When the new subject appears more than once, collect terms with that letter on one side, then factorise and divide.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Given</strong> \\(v = u + at\\), <strong>find \\(v\\) when \\(u = 5\\), \\(a = 3\\), \\(t = 4\\)</strong></p><p>\\(v = 5 + 3 \\times 4 = 5 + 12 = 17\\)</p>"
 },
